    "knowledge_point_id": "fa1ddb40-3494-541c-b23e-b15dd98bf669",
    "knowledge_point": "Identifying the Reason for a Discontinuity",
    "problem": "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=-1?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2}, & x<-1 \\\\ 2, & x=-1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}, & x>-1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
    "worked_solution": "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1.$\n\n- $f(-1)=2,$ so $f(-1)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}(\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}+1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{2} \\\\  & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-1)^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=1$ $✓$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=1,$ while $f(-1)=2.$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-1$ because ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1).$",
